---
/**
 * PricingSection — block 06, moved up from position 9 per the design
 * spec's §84 argument: a family should be able to answer "how much does
 * this cost?" within seconds, and transparent pricing is one of the three
 * brand pillars (see Difference.astro), so surfacing it early is itself
 * the trust argument.
 *
 * Never a hardcoded price string — every figure below comes from
 * packageTotalCents() + formatSgd(). PricingCard is where the itemised /
 * total-only branch actually lives; see that component for why no package
 * can grow an *invented* breakdown. The without-replacement package grew a
 * real one on 2026-08-16, supplied by DirectHired, which is why both cards
 * now itemise and the grid below is 1fr 1fr rather than 3fr 2fr.
 *
 * Design spec §6.4 names this block among the CTA surfaces that carry the
 * primary/secondary conversion pair "in that order everywhere, without
 * exception" — a family that has just resolved "how much does this
 * cost?" (§84's #1 objection) is at peak intent, so sending them only to
 * a static detail page would be a weaker ask than every other CTA moment
 * on the site. The pair renders exactly as in Hero.astro: primary
 * Submit Your Requirements, secondary WhatsApp Us, both sourced from
 * company.ts / lib/whatsapp.ts, never hardcoded. "View Pricing" stays as
 * a small tertiary text link below the pair — the escape hatch for the
 * minority who want the itemised detail before doing anything else.
 *
 * The lede must stay consistent with `.pricing-qualifier` below it. Brief
 * §19 forbids representing the package total as necessarily the complete
 * final employer cost — so "explained in full", "everything you pay", or
 * any equivalent completeness promise is contradicted by the qualifier
 * this very section renders. The lede instead surfaces the qualifier's
 * existence upfront, which is the honest and defensible version of the
 * claim.
 *
 * THE QUALIFIER WAS WRONG UNTIL 2026-08-16, and the correction is binding
 * on every surface, not just this one. It read "Additional helper
 * placement-related fees may apply depending on the selected helper and
 * placement arrangement" — two errors in one sentence. "May apply" makes a
 * certainty conditional, and stating a fee without stating that it is
 * recovered makes it read as a cost the employer bears. Core-pages design
 * spec §2.4: the employer ADVANCES the helper's loan and placement fee and
 * RECOVERS them through the helper's repayment; they are ultimately the
 * HELPER'S cost. Copy must carry both halves — the money leaves the
 * employer's account first, and it comes back — because either half on its
 * own is a different and untrue claim. The same correction was applied to
 * src/content/faq/cost.md, and /pricing's LoanAndPlacement section is
 * where the whole explanation lives, which is why the qualifier now links
 * there.
 *
 * The repayment mechanics themselves are NOT published anywhere on the
 * site — they are gated behind DirectHired's compliance sign-off (spec
 * §2.5, recorded in scripts/generate-info-required.mjs, enforced by
 * tests/compliance-gate.test.ts). Do not add them here.
 */
import Container from '../components/Container.astro'
import SectionHeader from '../components/SectionHeader.astro'
import PricingCard from '../components/PricingCard.astro'
import Button from '../components/Button.astro'
import { packages } from '../data/pricing'
import { company } from '../data/company'
import { defaultWhatsappUrl } from '../lib/whatsapp'

const whatsappHref = defaultWhatsappUrl()
---

<section class="pricing">
  <Container>
    <SectionHeader
      title="Transparent Pricing. No Guesswork."
      lede="Two ways to bring a helper into your home, with package costs set out upfront — and a plain account of what sits outside them."
    />

    <div class="pricing-grid">
      {packages.map((pkg) => <PricingCard pkg={pkg} />)}
    </div>

    <p class="pricing-qualifier">
      The helper’s loan and placement fee sit outside these packages. You advance both at the
      start and recover them through the helper’s repayment, so they are ultimately the helper’s
      cost rather than an extra charge you carry — <a href="/pricing">see how that works</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="pricing-cta">
      <div class="pricing-cta-buttons">
        <Button variant="primary" href={company.requirementFormUrl}>Submit Your Requirements</Button>
        <Button variant="secondary" href={whatsappHref} external>WhatsApp Us</Button>
      </div>
      <a class="pricing-view-link" href="/pricing">View Pricing</a>
    </div>
  </Container>
</section>

<style>
  .pricing {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /* --color-surface, not --color-surface-raised: this section renders
       Card via PricingCard, and Card itself is --color-surface-raised.
       A raised section background was identical to the card background
       it hosts — measured via getComputedStyle, not just the token
       names, since backgroundColor doesn't cascade and an unset section
       background can resolve to a different visible color than its own
       declared value would suggest. Zero rendered contrast left the
       cards separated only by a 1px border and shadow-sm (max alpha
       .06). See task-17-report.md's section-background audit. */
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .pricing-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
  }

  .pricing-qualifier {
    /*
     * 60ch keeps a readable measure — at 13px that computes to ~500px.
     * `text-wrap: balance` was added when this was a single sentence that
     * broke across two lines and orphaned three words on the second; it is
     * kept now that the §2.4 correction has made it three lines, where it
     * does the same job across all of them. `pretty` would only protect
     * the last line.
     */
    max-width: 60ch;
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-small);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* The qualifier's link into /pricing, where the whole explanation lives.
     --color-accent (6.03:1 on this section's cream ground), never
     --color-brand-teal, which fails AA for text. */
  .pricing-qualifier a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .pricing-qualifier a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent-hover);
  }

  .pricing-cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  .pricing-cta-buttons {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  /* Demoted, not a Button variant — small and muted on purpose, so the
     primary/secondary pair above reads as the section's real CTA and
     this stays the escape hatch for readers who want the full price
     list before doing anything else. */
  .pricing-view-link {
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-small);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .pricing-view-link:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  @media (min-width: 48em) {
    .pricing-grid {
      /*
       * Equal columns, because the two packages now carry equal content.
       *
       * This was 3fr/2fr while the without-replacement package was
       * `total-only` — a single number against six line items, where an
       * asymmetric split reflected real content weight. DirectHired
       * supplied that breakdown on 2026-08-16, so both cards now show six
       * line items and a total, and the asymmetry no longer describes
       * anything. Two packages a visitor is comparing should be presented
       * at the same weight; a wider card reads as the recommended one.
       *
       * If a future package is ever total-only again, revisit this rather
       * than leaving a narrow card next to a wide one by accident.
       */
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      align-items: stretch;
    }
  }
</style>
